<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Australia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Australia is a continent and country in the southern hemisphere. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> began colonising it in 1788 with a penal settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a>, and several self-governing colonies developed in the nineteenth century, uniting as the Commonwealth of Australia in 1901. It became one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>'s major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> and settler societies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/australia.jpg" alt="Australia" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (Vector graphics image by Ian Fieggen) (only minor code changes by uploader.) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Australia_(converted).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Australia comprises the mainland of the Australian continent, the island of Tasmania and numerous smaller islands. It has a land area of 7,688,287 square kilometres, the sixth largest in the world, and the mainland is the world's smallest continent. About 70% of the landmass is arid or semi-arid, and the mainland is relatively flat. The Great Dividing Range runs along most of the east, and Mount Kosciuszko, at 2,228 metres, is the highest mountain on the mainland. The Murray-Darling is the major river system. The Great Barrier Reef, the world's largest coral reef, lies off the north-east coast and extends for more than 2,300 kilometres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human habitation is estimated to have begun 50,000 to 65,000 years ago, with migration from what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. Aboriginal Australian culture is one of the oldest continuous cultures on Earth. At first European contact <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-australians">Aboriginal Australians</a> belonged to a wide range of societies across at least 250 language groups, and estimates of their population before British settlement range from 300,000 to 3 million. Torres Strait Islander people first settled their islands at least 2,500 years ago and are culturally and linguistically distinct from mainland peoples. Trade had been established by the mid-18th century between Aboriginal groups in the north and Makassan trepangers visiting from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>.</p><p>In 1606 the crew of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> ship Duyfken, captained by Willem Janszoon, made the first documented European landing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abel-tasman">Abel Tasman</a>'s voyage of 1642 was the first known European expedition to reach Van Diemen's Land, and the Dutch named the continent New Holland. British colonisation began in 1788 with the penal colony of New South Wales. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lachlan-macquarie">Lachlan Macquarie</a> recommended the name Australia in December 1817. By 1890 each of six self-governing colonies had gained responsible government, and they federated in 1901 as the Commonwealth of Australia. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia-act-1986">Australia Act 1986</a> completed the process of increasing autonomy from the United Kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Australia has a population of 27,801,023, heavily concentrated on the east coast and highly urbanised, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canberra">Canberra</a> as capital and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney">Sydney</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> as the most populous cities. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a> and a federation of six states and ten territories, with a constitution mostly unchanged since 1901. The white Australia policy, strengthened after Federation, ended in 1973, and immigrants now account for 30% of the population. Indigenous art includes more than 100,000 Aboriginal rock art sites.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Australia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its later relative decline from a position comparable to that of Canada and <b>Australia</b> became a classic puzzle of economic history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It linked the agricultural resources of India, the plantation economies of the Caribbean and later West Africa and Malaya, the mineral wealth of <b>Australia</b> and South Africa, and the manufacturing output of industrial Britain within a sterling-based trading and monetary system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…first self-governing dominion of the British Empire, and its gradual, negotiated path from colony to independence, through responsible government, Confederation and the Statute of Westminster, became a model for <b>Australia</b>, New Zealand and South Africa and for the later transformation of the empire into the Commonwealth of Nations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Commonwealth of <b>Australia</b> grew out of six British colonies founded between 1788, when the First Fleet established a penal settlement at Sydney Cove, and 1859, when Queensland was separated from New South Wales, on a continent inhabited by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for at least…</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>China, the DPRK and the Soviet Union gave aid and troops; against them the United States, <b>Australia</b>, the Republic of Korea, Thailand and New Zealand supported the South.</blockquote><a href="/empire/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The one great external contest, with a far stronger <b>Australia</b> over the oil and gas of the Timor Sea, was fought by public diplomacy and international law and settled by a maritime boundary treaty in 2018.</blockquote><a href="/empire/east-timor">East Timor · Military</a></li></ul></section>
